# Upstream timeouts and headers (P1.28; plan §6.1 Deadlines; invariant 3). Caddy v2.11.7 reverse_proxy and the
# servers timeouts option (caddyserver/website src/docs/markdown/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy.md, "Headers" and
# "The http transport"; options.md, "timeouts").
#
#
# The upstream gives up after 130 s (limits.json EDGE_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_S), above the largest route deadline (120 s,
# P1.04's deadlineMs cap) and REQUEST_DEADLINE_MS (at most 60 s), so the app's own 503 http.deadline always reaches the
# client first; the server's write timeout (135 s, in the Caddyfile) sits above it in turn.

# `reverse_proxy` with the client address withheld: the client's own forwarding headers are dropped, so the upstream
# sees only the edge's socket address (the PDS stores what it is given; P1b-A1, Alex's "Do not forward"). Caddy
# v2.11.7 sets X-Forwarded-For before applying header_up (reverseproxy.go:909 and 714), so the removal wins; it passes
# Host and X-Forwarded-Host by default.
(upstream) {
	reverse_proxy {args[0]} {
		header_up -X-Forwarded-For
		header_up -X-Real-IP
		header_up -Forwarded
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		transport http {
			dial_timeout 5s
			response_header_timeout 130s
		}
	}
}
